title: Trash
kind: overview
aliases: trash-bin delete
summary: A per-repository bin: trashing a file sets its bytes aside and takes its metarecords with them, until you restore or prune it.

Every metafolder operation that would delete or overwrite a file goes through the
repository's trash-bin instead: deleting a tracked file by hand, a rollback that
would overwrite a file, a sync (spec-sync). No byte is lost by accident: you
decide later whether to bring it back or to discard it.

A trashing takes the ''metarecord'' with the bytes — and for a directory, every
metarecord under it. They leave the repository (queries, counts and listings no
longer see them) and live in the trash entry until it is restored, which puts
them back at their original uuids, or pruned, which is where both die. The trash
is filesystem state managed by the clients (`mf` and the GUI); the daemon only
deletes the metarecords, through one endpoint.

